'''Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''' is a [[Baptists|Baptist]] church in the [[Buckhead]] district of [[Atlanta]], Georgia, United States. It was established in 1932 through the merger of Second Baptist Church and Ponce de Leon Baptist Church (established 1904). Its current church building was dedicated in 1937. It is affiliated with both the [[Cooperative Baptist Fellowship]] and the [[Southern Baptist Convention]] (through its state affiliate, the [[Georgia Baptist Convention]]).

The church traces its history to 1854, when Second Baptist Church was established in [[downtown Atlanta]]. Ponce de Leon Baptist Church was established in 1904. In the early 1930s, both churches saw a decline in their congregations and experienced financial difficulties brought about by the [[Great Depression in the United States|Great Depression]], prompting the two churches to merge and relocate. Since the mid-20th century, the church has had one of the largest congregations in the Southern Baptist Convention.

The current church building was designed by architect [[George Harwell Bond]] in the [[Georgian architecture|Georgian Colonial]] and [[Colonial Revival architecture|neocolonial]] style, with inspiration drawn from [[Colonial meeting house|colonial meeting houses]] and churches of that era.

== History ==

=== Predecessor churches ===
Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church was formed as the result of the merger of two [[Atlanta]]-based Baptist churches: Second Baptist Church and Ponce de Leon Baptist Church.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Allen |first=Bob |date=September 11, 2013 |title=McAfee professor takes Atlanta pastorate |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/mcafee-professor-takes-atlanta-pastorate/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618122855/https://baptistnews.com/article/mcafee-professor-takes-atlanta-pastorate/#.YMyRl2TP32c |archive-date=June 18, 2021 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> Both of these churches had been established as [[Christian mission#Baptist missions|missions]] of Atlanta's [[First Baptist Church (Atlanta)|First Baptist Church]], which had been established in 1848.<ref name=":0" />

==== Second Baptist Church ====
[[File:Second Baptist Church of Atlanta plaque.jpg|thumb|279x279px|Historical marker at the location of Second Baptist Church near the [[Georgia State Capitol]]]]
Second Baptist was established on September 1, 1854, by 19 members of First Baptist.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=25}} [[The Reverend]] Charles M. Irwin served as the church's first [[Pastor#Baptist|pastor]].{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} For the first several months after its establishment, Second Baptist operated as a [[house church]] while construction of its [[Church (building)|church building]] was underway.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|pp=25–26}} The church would be located at the intersection of [[Mitchell Street (Atlanta)|Mitchell Street]] and Washington Street,{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|pp=25–26}} across the street from the [[Atlanta City Hall]].{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=37}}{{refn|group=note|Atlanta City Hall later relocated, with the former site of the city hall serving as the location for the current [[Georgia State Capitol]].{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=37}}}} Work was completed in 1858, with the building being the first brick church built in Atlanta.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} During construction, a man named James Clark donated $500 to the church for the creation of a [[church bell]], which was [[Casting|cast]] in the English city of [[Sheffield]] by [[Naylor Vickers & Co.]] in 1860.{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|pp=37-–38}}

In 1864, during the [[Atlanta campaign]] of the [[American Civil War]], the church's bell was rung as a warning to city residents of the arrival of [[Union Army]] forces under the command of General [[William Tecumseh Sherman]].{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=38}} In the fall of 1864, services ceased due to an evacuation from the city, but resumed by April 1865.{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=37}} In the interim, the building was used as a [[warehouse]] for [[dry goods]] and furniture.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}}{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=37}}

The building was spared from destruction thanks to the advocacy of [[Thomas O'Reilly (priest)|Thomas O'Reilly]],{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} a [[Catholic priest]] whose church was located on the same [[city block]] as Second Baptist.{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=37}} A 1939 editorial in ''[[The Atlanta Journal]]'' described the church and its bell as "one of the few landmarks that went unscathed when the flames of destruction roared".{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=38}} Shortly after the war's end in 1865, a [[Private school#United States|private school]] began holding classes in the basement of the church.{{sfn|Shavin|Galphin|1985|p=81}} Religious services continued at the church's original building until 1890, when it was demolished to make way for a new church building.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} This new building was completed by 1893.{{sfn|Kunkle|1989|p=41}} In 1909, the church had a membership of 1,351.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=2}}

Several other churches in Atlanta began as missions of Second Baptist.{{sfn|Shavin|Galphin|1985|p=37}} These include Third Baptist (also known as Jones Avenue Baptist Church),{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|pp=28–29}} Fourth Baptist (also known as James Chapel before merging with another church to become Central Baptist),{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=29}} and Immanuel Baptist.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|pp=40–41}} Notable members of Second Baptist included four [[Governor of Georgia|governors of Georgia]] ([[Joseph E. Brown]], [[Joseph Mackey Brown]], [[Henry Dickerson McDaniel]], and [[Joseph M. Terrell]]), [[List of mayors of Atlanta|Atlanta Mayor]] [[George Hillyer]], Judge [[Beverly Daniel Evans Jr.]], and academic [[Marion L. Brittain]].{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=20}}

==== Ponce de Leon Baptist Church ====
Ponce de Leon Baptist Church was established on October 6, 1904, with 115 charter members.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=40}} Of these, 84 were from First Baptist, while 19 were from Second Baptist.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=40}} Prior to the creation of a dedicated church building, services were initially held at a house on [[Peachtree Street]] before using the facilities of [[North Avenue Presbyterian Church]].{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=40}} Prior to the arrival of the church's first pastor in January 1905, former Georgia Governor [[William J. Northen]] served as the congregation's chairman.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=40}} Property at the intersection of [[Piedmont Avenue (Atlanta)|Piedmont Avenue]] and [[Ponce de Leon Avenue]] was purchased by the church as the site for a permanent building, with the first services in this new facility being held in September 1906.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=40}}
[[File:Ponce de Leon Baptist Church, 1909.png|thumb|Ponce de Leon Baptist Church, 1909|left]]
In September 1908, the Stone Mountain Baptist Association held a meeting at the church that ultimately led to the creation of the Atlanta Baptist Association the following year.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=1}} At that time, the church had a membership of 376.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=3}} In 1929, the church purchased property along Peachtree Road for the construction of a new church building, which they moved into the following year.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}}{{refn|group=note|Both Peachtree Street and Peachtree Road refer to the same thoroughfare in different areas of the city.}}

Also in 1929, the church merged with Buckhead Baptist Church, which had been established in 1911.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} For several months after this merger, the church held services in Buckhead Baptist's building while construction commenced on the Peachtree Road property.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Much of the history of Buckhead Baptist is unknown, as its historical records were destroyed in a [[Structure fire|warehouse fire]] sometime prior to 1954.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|pp=36–37}}

=== Merger ===
By the early 1930s, the congregations at both Second and Ponce de Leon began to decline as people began to relocate outside [[downtown Atlanta]].{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}} Additionally, both churches were experiencing economic stress caused by the [[Great Depression in the United States]].{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=204}} As a result, the two churches decided that it would be in their best interests to merge and relocate to the [[Buckhead]] district of Atlanta.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}} This merger occurred in November 1932,{{refn|group=note|name=Founding_date}} with the new church adopting its current name.<ref name=":0" />{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Architect [[George Harwell Bond]] designed a new building for the combined congregation,{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=242}} with construction finishing in 1937.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}} Construction on the church coincided with a slight development boom in the [[Garden Hills]] neighborhood of Buckhead, occurring around the same time that [[Philip T. Shutze]]'s [[North Fulton High School (Georgia)|North Fulton High School]] and [[Garden Hills Elementary School]] were being constructed.{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=243}} On October 10, 1937, the church was officially [[Dedication of churches#Baptist and Pentecostal forms|dedicated]].{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Two years later, on December 12, 1939, the church bell that had been installed in the original Second Baptist building was installed in the new building.{{sfn|''Atlanta Historical Bulletin''|1945|p=38}}

In 1941, the church was surveyed as part of a project by the [[Works Progress Administration]].{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Surveyors listed that, at the time, the church had a membership of 2,060 and a [[Sunday school]] enrollment of 1,607.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} The church's property was given a [[Real estate appraisal|valuation]] of $425,000 (equivalent to ${{Inflation|index=US|value=0.425|start_year=1941|r=2}} million in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}).{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} At the time, Ryland Knight served as the pastor.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Knight had previously been the pastor at Second Baptist and continued in that role at Second-Ponce de Leon until 1945.{{sfn|Newton|1958|p=20}}

=== Leadership of Monroe F. Swilley Jr. ===
On October 1, 1945,{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=5}} Monroe F. Swilley Jr. became Second-Ponce de Leon's pastor.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Falk |first=Jennifer |date=September 22, 2023 |title=Swilley founded college that would become University's Atlanta campus |url=https://den.mercer.edu/swilley-founded-college-that-would-become-universitys-atlanta-campus-mercer-legends/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250724004500/https://den.mercer.edu/swilley-founded-college-that-would-become-universitys-atlanta-campus-mercer-legends/ |archive-date=July 24, 2025 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |website=The Den |publisher=[[Mercer University]]}}</ref> At the time, the church had a congregation of about 2,500.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=5}} In the late 1940s, he oversaw an expansion plan for the church, purchasing property adjacent to the church as well as a new property north of the church to serve as the location for a new church.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=5}} Physical changes to the church's campus included the creation of a [[chapel]] building, a recreation building, and a [[Clergy house|pastorium]].{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=5}} By 1954, the congregation had grown to about 3,600, with a Sunday school enrollment of 3,500.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=5}} Under his pastorship, the church's congregation became one of the largest churches in not only the [[Georgia Baptist Convention]], but the [[Southern Baptist Convention]] as a whole.<ref name=":1" />
[[File:Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church sanctuary, circa 1954.png|thumb|256x256px|The [[nave]] of the church, {{Circa|1954}}]]
In 1950, Swilley was appointed by the Atlanta Baptist Association to chair a committee tasked with established a Baptist college in [[Metro Atlanta]].<ref name=":1" /> The congregants of Second-Ponce de Leon were largely supportive of the proposed college and contributed extensively to fundraising projects.<ref name=":1" /> In 1969, the Atlanta Baptist College opened, with Swilley leaving Second-Ponce de Leon in order to serve as the college's first president.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Honaker |first=Andrea |date=October 30, 2024 |title=Mercerians lead Atlanta church's ministry team |url=https://den.mercer.edu/mercerians-lead-atlanta-churchs-ministry-team/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710203156/https://den.mercer.edu/mercerians-lead-atlanta-churchs-ministry-team/ |archive-date=July 10, 2025 |access-date=August 28, 2025 |website=The Den |publisher=[[Mercer University]]}}</ref> In 1972, the college merged with [[Mercer University]], with Swilley serving as a vice president until retiring in 1989.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":4" />
For the first several decades after its creation, Second-Ponce de Leon's congregation and [[clergy]] was made up entirely of [[White Americans]].{{sfn|Scott|2020|pp=3–4}} In 1961, the church's [[Deacon#Baptists|deacons]] established a [[Racial segregation in the United States|racially segregated]] seating plan whereby any [[African Americans|African American]] visitors would sit in a roped-off area in the rear of the sanctuary.{{sfn|Scott|2020|pp=3–4}}

=== Late 20th century ===
In the 1970s, [[Russell H. Dilday]] served as pastor for the church.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Wingfield |first=Mark |last2=Mwaura |first2=Maina |date=April 17, 2023 |title=Charles Marsh has a story to tell of evangelical anxiety |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/charles-marsh-has-a-story-to-tell-of-evangelical-anxiety/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241204132044/https://baptistnews.com/article/charles-marsh-has-a-story-to-tell-of-evangelical-anxiety/ |archive-date=December 4, 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> This was a period of increasing strife between conservative and liberal factions in the Southern Baptist Convention.{{sfn|Fletcher|1994|p=247}} 38 deacons left First Baptist Church in the decade after Charles Stanley, a strong conservative, became pastor of that church.{{sfn|Fletcher|1994|p=247}} Many of these deacons joined the congregation of Second-Ponce de Leon,{{sfn|Fletcher|1994|p=247}} which ultimately accepted about 200 former members of First Baptist.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wingfield |first=Mark |date=April 18, 2023 |title=Charles Stanley shaped Christian broadcasting and the Southern Baptist Convention |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/charles-stanley-shaped-christian-broadcasting-and-the-southern-baptist-convention/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241210052213/https://baptistnews.com/article/charles-stanley-shaped-christian-broadcasting-and-the-southern-baptist-convention/ |archive-date=December 10, 2024 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> Addressing the division in 1976, Dilday told his congregation that the attention of the Southern Baptist Convention should not "be wasted on differences in theological positions but rather spent on unity and usefulness in the task God has given us to carry out".<ref name=":5" /> Dilday departed from Second-Ponce de Leon in 1978 to become the president of the [[Southwestern Baptist Theological Seminary]] in [[Fort Worth, Texas]].<ref name=":5" />{{sfn|Fletcher|1994|p=255}}

Dilday was succeeded in 1979 by Robert Marsh, who attempted to lead the church in a [[Moderate Christianity|moderate]] position.<ref name=":5" /> In 1986, the church hosted a meeting of a Southern Baptist Convention [[Women in ministry|Women in Ministry]] organization.{{sfn|Bellinger|1993|p=137}} In 1990, an addition to the church building was added, with the [[architectural firm]] of Peter Hand and Associates overseeing the project.{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=242}} In 1995, the church introduced an outreach program to help certain groups in the community, including cancer patients, the homeless, prisoners, immigrants, students, and prayer groups.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}} By 1999, the church boasted a congregation of about 3,600 members and was affiliated with both the Southern Baptist Convention and the [[Cooperative Baptist Fellowship]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |date=March 25, 1999 |title=Minister with Children |url=https://archive.org/details/19990325 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |work=The Baptist Record |publisher=[[Mississippi Baptist Convention]] |page=10 |volume=123 |issue=7}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |date=April 1, 1999 |title=Minister with Children |url=https://archive.org/details/19990401 |access-date=August 9, 2025 |work=The Baptist Record |publisher=[[Mississippi Baptist Convention]] |page=6 |volume=123 |issue=8}}</ref> At the time, the church offered both contemporary and traditional worship services and accepted both men and women to the diaconate.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

=== 21st century ===

In 2003, the church had a membership of about 3,500,{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}} making it one of the largest Southern Baptist congregations in the United States.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=81}} Among the programs offered by the church at this time were a weekday preschool program and a recreational facility that was open to both church members and non-members.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=204}} At the time, the church was in the midst of a $12 million (${{Inflation|index=US|value=12|start_year=2003|r=0}} million in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}) renovation project.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=204}} In January 2004, the church hosted [[Billy Kim]] and [[Denton Lotz]], the president and general secretary of the [[Baptist World Alliance]], respectively, as part of a series of meetings that the two were having with moderate Southern Baptist churches to garner support for their group.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 29, 2004 |title=BWA president Kim says 'liberalism' is in eye of beholder |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/bwa-president-kim-says-liberalism-is-in-eye-of-beholder/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201233347/https://baptistnews.com/article/bwa-president-kim-says-liberalism-is-in-eye-of-beholder/ |archive-date=December 1, 2022 |access-date=August 27, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> In 2009, the church hosted the [[funeral]] services for former [[United States Attorney General]] [[Griffin Bell]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 5, 2009 |title=Griffin Bell, former Carter official, Baptist statesman, dies |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/griffin-bell-former-carter-official-baptist-statesman-dies/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828001501/https://baptistnews.com/article/griffin-bell-former-carter-official-baptist-statesman-dies/ |archive-date=August 28, 2025 |access-date=August 28, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref>

In the 2010s, the church was involved in several projects with other Baptist churches. In March 2010, the church hosted the unveiling of the ''Celebrating Grace Hymnal'', a Baptist [[hymnal]] developed by Mercer University in collaboration with individuals from several Baptist churches and religious institutions.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 8, 2010 |title=New Baptist hymnal unveiled in Atlanta |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/new-baptist-hymnal-unveiled-in-atlanta-2/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828000236/https://baptistnews.com/article/new-baptist-hymnal-unveiled-in-atlanta-2/ |archive-date=August 28, 2025 |access-date=August 28, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> In November of the following year, the church hosted the [[New Baptist Covenant]] II, an ecumenical meeting of Baptists from different denominations and racial and ethnic backgrounds.<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 23, 2022 |title=New Baptist Covenant II to unite Baptists personally and virtually |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/new-baptist-covenant-ii-to-unite-baptists-personally-and-virtually/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220125164720/https://baptistnews.com/article/new-baptist-covenant-ii-to-unite-baptists-personally-and-virtually/#.YfAppqTP32c |archive-date=January 25, 2022 |access-date=August 28, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 23, 2011 |title=St. Louis 'Covenant' event opens with worship, ends with ministry |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/st-louis-covenant-event-opens-with-worship-ends-with-ministry/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250827235719/https://baptistnews.com/article/st-louis-covenant-event-opens-with-worship-ends-with-ministry/ |archive-date=August 27, 2025 |access-date=August 27, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> Former [[President of the United States|United States President]] [[Jimmy Carter]], himself a Baptist, participated in the event, speaking at the church.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 17, 2011 |title=Carter notes progress in building Baptist unity, breaking barriers |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/carternotesprogressinbuildingbaptistunitybreakingbarriers/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250827234942/https://baptistnews.com/article/carternotesprogressinbuildingbaptistunitybreakingbarriers/ |archive-date=August 27, 2025 |access-date=August 27, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 18, 2011 |title=Jimmy Carter notes progress in building Baptist unity |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/jimmy-carter-notes-progress-in-building-baptist-unity/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828001110/https://baptistnews.com/article/jimmy-carter-notes-progress-in-building-baptist-unity/ |archive-date=August 28, 2025 |access-date=August 28, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref>

In 2019, the church launched a program aimed at promoting diversity within the church.{{sfn|Scott|2020|pp=102–103}} However, as of 2020, the makeup of the church was still largely white and, due in part to its location in the more affluent Atlanta district of Buckhead, wealthier than average.{{sfn|Scott|2020|p=3}} That year, the church established a college ministry.{{sfn|Scott|2020|p=104}} In 2021, the church was one of several hosts for the Cooperating Baptist Fellowship's annual General Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 1, 2021 |title=CBF details plans for hybrid General Assembly in August |url=https://baptistnews.com/article/cbf-details-plans-for-hybrid-general-assembly-in-august/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331123358/https://baptistnews.com/article/cbf-details-plans-for-hybrid-general-assembly-in-august/ |archive-date=March 31, 2023 |access-date=August 27, 2025 |website=[[Baptist News Global]]}}</ref> At the time, the church was still dually affiliated with the Cooperating Baptist Fellowship and Southern Baptist Convention.{{sfn|Scott|2020|pp=3–4}}

== Architecture and design ==
[[File:Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church, circa 1954.png|thumb|Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church, {{Circa|1954}}|282x282px]]The church is located in the Garden Hills section of Buckhead,{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=243}} at 2715 Peachtree Road NE.{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=204}}{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=242}} It is located in close proximity to two other large churches that are also on Peachtree Road: the [[Episcopal Cathedral of Saint Philip (Atlanta)|Episcopal Cathedral of Saint Philip]], which serves as the cathedral for the [[Episcopal Diocese of Atlanta]], and the [[Cathedral of Christ the King (Atlanta)|Cathedral of Christ the King]], which serves as the cathedral for the [[Roman Catholic]] [[Archdiocese of Atlanta]].{{sfn|Hickel|2003|p=82}}

The church building is four stories tall and made of brick, with a white painted exterior.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} The building has a [[frontage]] of {{Convert|71|ft|m}} and a length of {{Convert|167|ft|m|abbr=on}}.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} The auditorium has dimensions of {{Convert|68|ft|m|abbr=on}} by {{Convert|87|ft|m|abbr=on}}, with a balcony on either side of the room that extends {{Convert|13|ft|m|abbr=on}} inwards.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} The height from the ground to the ceiling at the pulpit is {{Convert|36|ft|m|abbr=on}}.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} Measured from ground level, the spire atop the steeple reaches a height of {{Convert|160|ft|m|abbr=on}}.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}}{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}}

A 1941 survey of the building reported that it contained 70 rooms.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} Per Bond, the auditorium could hold a total of 1,274, with 812 seated in the main floor area, 418 in the balcony area, and 44 in the choir.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} Additionally, the total capacity for all of the Sunday school rooms is approximately 1,500.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}}

Concerning the [[architectural style]], a 1941 survey by the Works Progress Administration reported that the building was constructed in the [[Georgian Colonial]] style.{{sfn|The Georgia Historical Records Survey|1941|p=26}} In a 1993 book, the [[American Institute of Architects]] referred to the style as [[Neocolonial architecture|neocolonial]], with the addition to the structure being in the [[Postmodern architecture|postmodern]] style.{{sfn|Gournay|1993|p=243}} Bond stated that he drew inspiration from [[Colonial meeting house|colonial meeting houses]] and churches from that era.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} For the exterior, he specifically highlighted [[First Baptist Church in America]] in Providence, Rhode Island, [[Trinity Church (Newport, Rhode Island)|Trinity Church]] in Newport, Rhode Island, and both [[Old North Church]] and [[Old South Church]] in Boston.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}} For the interior, he highlighted [[Old Whaler's Church (Sag Harbor)|First Presbyterian Church]] in Sag Harbor, New York, [[Old First Presbyterian Church (Newark, New Jersey)|First Presbyterian Church]] in Newark, New Jersey, and others.{{sfn|''A History of the Second-Ponce de Leon Baptist Church''|1954|p=3}}

